7.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: I think the C V C + final {b,d,t,w} reading is the one that actually captures why dad/dab/gut/low/cat go together and why ear/cop/ego/mom/ate are quoted as breaks. 

dad, dab, gut, low, cat are all C V C with medial a/o/u and final b/d/t/w. That is exactly the family that colleagues 2,3,5,6 are describing. On that description rat = r a t is a textbook continuation: C V C, medial a, final t ∈ {b,d,t,w}. It is the only one of the five options that keeps the family intact, which is why it keeps coming up as the ‘good’ member.

That makes the odd-one-out task a little over-determined, because we have four clear non-members in the option set:

* leg = L E G – medial e, not a/o/u, and final g ∉ {b,d,t,w};
* dam = D A M – medial a is fine but final m ∉ {b,d,t,w};
* car = C A R – medial a is fine but final r ∉ {b,d,t,w};
* bin = B I N – medial i, not a/o/u, and final n ∉ {b,d,t,w}.

Leg is the most canonical break. It is the only option that brings in the e-vowel that the negative exemplars ear/ego/ate are used to flag, and it also breaks the final-consonant set with g. That is the same kind of violation that the quoted non-examples are meant to illustrate. Dam also breaks the final-consonant set, with m as in mom, and car/bin break the final-consonant set in the same way.

The e/r/p/m-avoidance reading raised by 8 and 9 is useful for seeing the letter inventory, but on that reading leg, dam, rat and car are all non-pattern and bin is the only option that is inventory-clean. That multiplicity is why a unique odd-one-out has to be chosen by salience, and the salient e-middle violation is leg. That is why A. leg is the natural ‘does not follow’ choice here.

5.text:
A. leg

The five positives are all C V C with medial vowel in {a,o,u} and final consonant in {b,d,t,w}

* dad  C a C  final d
* dab  C a C  final b
* gut  C u C  final t
* low  C o C  final w
* cat  C a C  final t

The quoted negatives break that description by shape or by final / vowel

* ear V V C
* cop C o C final p ∉ {b,d,t,w}
* ego V C V
* mom C o C final m ∉ {b,d,t,w}
* ate V C V

On that C V C / vowel {a,o,u} / final {b,d,t,w} description

* leg  L E G  medial e ∉ {a,o,u} and final g ∉ {b,d,t,w}
* dam  D A M  final m ∉ {b,d,t,w}
* car  C A R  final r ∉ {b,d,t,w}
* bin  B I N  medial i ∉ {a,o,u} and final n ∉ {b,d,t,w}

all fail, whereas rat = R A T is C V C, medial a, final t ∈ {b,d,t,w} and is the only
option that would continue the dad/dab/gut/low/cat family.  Leg is the canonical
non-member, the e-containing C V C that is the analogue of ear/ego/ate.

3.text:
The five exemplars are all C V C and they are all C V C with a final
consonant in

{d , b , t , w}

dad = D A D   dab = D A B   gut = G U T   low = L O W   cat = C A T

and the two C V C counter-examples are exactly the C V C words that break
that final-consonant condition

cop = C O P   final p∉{b,d,t,w}
mom = C O M   final m∉{b,d,t,w}

ear , ego , ate are the shape counter-examples, V V C / V C V.

On the minimal C V C + final ∈{b,d,t,w} reading that is forced by the
data

* leg = L E G   final g∉{b,d,t,w} and medial e∉{a,o,u}
* dam = D A M   final m∉{b,d,t,w}
* car = C A R   final r∉{b,d,t,w}
* bin = B I N   medial i∉{a,o,u}, final n∉{b,d,t,w}

are all non-members, whereas

rat = R A T   C V C, medial a, final t∈{b,d,t,w}

is the only option that continues the dad/dab/gut/low/cat family.

Leg is the most canonical of the non-members: it is C V C in shape but
it brings in the vowel e which is the hallmark of the quoted
negatives ear/ego/ate and it breaks the final-consonant condition
g∉{b,d,t,w}.  In the usual ‘no e,m,p,r’ inventory reading this is the
first option that is ruled out, and it remains a non-member under
every reasonable formalisation of the pattern.

